    "innovation": "This paper, for the first time, models daily cod egg occurrences as a spawning proxy across the North Atlantic, revealing how climate change drives different phenological shifts and outcomes in western and eastern cod stocks.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Sea Surface Temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Sea Surface Temperature (SST, K converted into \u00b0C) originated from the ERA5 hourly reanalysis data and were downloaded at the Copernicus Climate Data Store (CDS) website (https://cds.climate.copernicus.eu/datasets). The data (1940-2021) had a resolution of 0.25\u00b0 \u00d7 0.25\u00b0.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Sea Ice Concentration",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Sea Ice Concentration (SIC, Dimensionless), referred to as Sea-Ice Cover, originated from the same ERA5 database as SST. They exhibited the same spatial resolution (i.e. 0.25\u00b0 \u00d7 0.25\u00b0).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ORAS5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Sea Surface Salinity",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Sea Surface Salinity (SSS, PSU) originated from the ORAS5 global ocean reanalysis monthly data and were downloaded at the Copernicus Climate Data Store (CDS) website. They were characterised by a spatial resolution of 0.25\u00b0 \u00d7 0.25\u00b0 (1958-2022).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CMIP6 Multi-Model ensemble Averaging",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Surface chlorophyll-a",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Surface chlorophyll-a (Chlos, kg.m-3 converted into mg.m-\u00b3) originated from the Deutsches KlimaRechenZentrum (DKRZ) node at the Earth System Grid Federation (ESGF) website (https://esgf-metagrid.cloud.dkrz.de/search). The 0.5\u00b0 \u00d7 0.5\u00b0 resolution final matrix resulted from averaging four Earth System Models (ESMs; Supplementary Fig.7), which correlated better with merged satellite data of GlobColour project (https://hermes.acri.fr; Supplementary Table 5). These CMIP6 data included historical records prior to 2014 and Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSP) middle of the road (SSP 2.45) after the subsequent period. The decision of averaging different Chlos models, referred to as Multi-Model ensemble Averaging (MMA approach), allowed us to improve performance while mitigating bias and variability within individual models68. Access information available prior to 1997 also guided our decision to choose a modeled product type.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Our model revealed that the AMO (an indicator of the Atlantic Multidecadal Variability47, or AMV) may have affected the phenology of cod spawning in the North Atlantic and its adjacent seas. Although a debate exists on whether or not the AMO is driven by internal hydro-climatic variability48, the patterns of past AMO-like SST variations are real49. Therefore, it is likely that the relationship between cod egg habitat suitability and AMO is driven by SST; note that SSTs are detrended prior to the calculation of the AMO index43. Because the AMO is unlikely to be an internal source of hydro-climatic variability48, a return to a negative phase cannot be expected in the context of anthropogenic global climate change49.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern Newfoundland",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A shift in cod egg habitat suitability anomalies was observed after the mid-1990s when the Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation (AMO) shifted from a negative (i.e. negative temperature anomalies) to a positive (i.e. positive temperature anomalies) state (Fig.3C-D). In eastern Newfoundland, the shift in cod egg habitat suitability anomalies corresponded to a decrease in summer anomalies that paralleled an increase in spring anomalies (Fig.3C).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A shift in cod egg habitat suitability anomalies was observed after the mid-1990s when the Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation (AMO) shifted from a negative (i.e. negative temperature anomalies) to a positive (i.e. positive temperature anomalies) state (Fig.3C-D). In eastern Newfoundland, the shift in cod egg habitat suitability anomalies corresponded to a decrease in summer anomalies that paralleled an increase in spring anomalies (Fig.3C). In the North Sea, where the spawning season is more restricted, the model only anticipated a reduction in suitability anomalies. Therefore, despite widespread negative cod egg suitability anomalies after the mid-1990s, a phenological compensation may still occur in eastern Newfoundland in contrast to the North Sea.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
